code.code:
async function loadDashboard() {
  const profile = await fetch('/api/profile').then((r) => r.json());
  const notifications = await fetch('/api/notifications').then((r) => r.json());
  const tasks = await fetch('/api/tasks').then((r) => r.json());
  render({ profile, notifications, tasks });
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Problems. (1) Request waterfall: three independent operations are awaited one at a time, so the route waits the sum of the latencies instead of the maximum; independent operations should be started before awaiting them. (2) No response.ok check: an HTTP 500 still resolves and its error payload is rendered as data. (3) No cancellation: nothing aborts the fetches when the route unmounts or changes, so the closure keeps working for a screen that no longer exists. (4) No stale-result guard: on fast re-entry, a slower older loadDashboard can resolve after the newer one and overwrite its data, and that happens while the route is mounted, so a mounted check alone would not fix it. (5) No error handling: any rejection is unhandled.

Rewrite:

let currentController: AbortController | undefined;

async function loadDashboard() {
  currentController?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  currentController = controller;
  const { signal } = controller;

  const responses = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/profile', { signal }),
    fetch('/api/notifications', { signal }),
    fetch('/api/tasks', { signal }),
  ]);
  const [profile, notifications, tasks] = await Promise.all(
    responses.map((response) => {
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
      return response.json();
    }),
  );

  if (currentController === controller) {
    render({ profile, notifications, tasks });
  }
}

loadDashboard().catch((error) => {
  if (error.name !== 'AbortError') showError(error);
});

function onRouteCleanup() {
  currentController?.abort();
  currentController = undefined;
}

Why each piece is there: Promise.all is correct because the screen requires every result; it preserves input order and rejects on the first rejection, but that rejection does not cancel the siblings, which is why one AbortSignal is passed to every fetch and aborted on cleanup. AbortError is treated as expected, not surfaced as an error. The currentController identity check is the stale-result guard: only the load that is still current may render, so a slow obsolete response can never overwrite newer data. If partial data were useful, replace Promise.all with Promise.allSettled and render each outcome independently.

code.code:
function debounce(fn: Function, wait: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  return function (...args: unknown[]) {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(fn, wait);
  };
}

const onSearch = debounce((query: string) => {
  api.search(query).then(renderResults);
}, 300);
searchInput.addEventListener('input', () => onSearch(searchInput.value));

const onScroll = debounce(() => {
  const y = window.scrollY;
  badge.style.transform = `translateY(${y}px)`;
}, 100);
window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll);

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Defects. (1) Arguments and this are dropped: the trailing call is setTimeout(fn, wait), so fn runs with no arguments and the searched query is undefined; the collected ...args are thrown away. (2) No cancellation: there is no cancel(), so a pending trailing call cannot be stopped, and on teardown the timer still fires, rendering results for a widget that is gone while the closure retains the stale work. (3) No leading edge or maxWait: a continuous typing burst postpones the search until the burst ends, which fast typists read as lag; the API must specify leading and trailing behavior and whether a maximum wait is required. (4) Wrong tool for the scroll badge: debouncing a visual handler only reduces call count; a timer callback is a task and is not frame-aligned, so the scrollY read and the style write can interleave between frames. (5) The intervals are magic numbers; the right delay is a product decision, not a universal constant.

Production version (trailing edge, argument- and this-forwarding, cancellable):

function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => void>(fn: T, wait: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  const debounced = function (this: unknown, ...args: Parameters<T>) {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
  debounced.cancel = () => {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = undefined;
  };
  return debounced;
}

Contract to document: trailing-edge only unless a leading option is added; maxWait if bursts must be bounded; arguments and this are forwarded; cancel() must be called on component teardown so no trailing call survives unmount. For the scroll badge, keep the handler light and batch the read and the write inside requestAnimationFrame so all reads happen before writes in a frame-aligned callback; a timer alone does not guarantee frame-aligned rendering. Debounce remains the right choice for the search because only the settled query matters; continuous scroll, resize, or pointer work is throttle territory, at most one invocation per interval.

code.code:
type User = { id: string; name: string; role: string };

function mergePages(pages: User[][]): User[] {
  return [...new Set(pages.flat())];
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
(1) Set membership uses SameValueZero, and for objects that means identity: values are compared by reference, not by structure (MDN Set, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Set). Each page is parsed separately, so the same user on two pages is two different object literals; neither equals the other, both are kept, and the spread preserves the input order with every duplicate intact. The insertion-order half of the Set reasoning is fine; the equality half is what fails.

(2) Last-wins rewrite keyed by the stable id:

function mergePages(pages: User[][]): User[] {
  const byId = new Map<string, User>();
  for (const page of pages) {
    for (const user of page) {
      byId.set(user.id, user);
    }
  }
  return [...byId.values()];
}

Map.set on an existing key replaces the value, and Map iteration follows the order in which each key was first inserted (MDN Map, https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Map), so the output is last-wins data at first-seen positions. If the rule were first-wins instead, guard with byId.has(user.id) before set, or use the Set-of-ids filter pattern.

(3) Complexity: O(n) average time for n records and O(n) additional space. The question to ask first is the conflict rule: first record wins, last record wins, or merge. It changes the data structure, not just a line, and it should be decided before coding.

