async function mapWithLimit<T, R>(
  items: readonly T[],
  limit: number,
  task: (item: T) => Promise<R>,
): Promise<R[]> {
  const results: R[] = [];
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (true) {
      const index = next++;
      if (index >= items.length) return;
      results.push(await task(items[index]));
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: limit }, worker));
  return results;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
(1) The order bug: results.push appends in completion order. Workers interleave at every await, so when item 2 resolves after item 5, its result lands after item 5's; whether that happens depends on network timing, which is why the order assertion fails intermittently instead of always. The fix is to store each result at its original index, not at the end of the array.

(2) Silent misbehavior for bad limits: limit = 0 or a negative number spawns zero workers, Promise.all of an empty array resolves immediately, and the function returns an empty array as if the work were done; a non-integer limit is silently truncated. The utility should validate that the limit is a positive integer and throw. The unstated failure contract: Promise.all over the workers rejects on the first worker error, but the other workers' in-flight tasks keep running; they are observed, not cancelled, and their results are never used. A scheduler must state fail-fast versus per-item outcomes, and whether already-started tasks are actively cancelled (thread an AbortSignal) or left to finish.

(3) Fixed version:

async function mapWithLimit<T, R>(
  items: readonly T[],
  limit: number,
  task: (item: T, index: number) => Promise<R>,
): Promise<R[]> {
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || limit < 1) throw new RangeError('limit');
  const results = new Array<R>(items.length);
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (true) {
      const index = next++;
      if (index >= items.length) return;
      results[index] = await task(items[index], index);
    }
  }

  await Promise.all(
    Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, worker),
  );
  return results;
}

Guarantees: at most limit tasks are in flight at any moment (the worker pool pulls indices; starting all tasks at once would be the unbounded Promise.all(items.map(task)) pattern this utility exists to prevent); output order equals input order regardless of completion order; the promise rejects on the first task error while already-started tasks are merely observed; invalid limits throw RangeError. The limit protects browser and server resources; it does not guarantee network throughput and does not make individual tasks faster.
